    "id": "b0331d27-c828-4016-95a4-e333ba3f0b84",
    "slug": "how-to-write-text-to-cad-prompts-that-actually-work",
    "title": "How to Write Text-to-CAD Prompts That Actually Work",
    "excerpt": "Prompt: \"Single-speed bicycle cog with HG splined bore, 7 mm base, weight-relief holes and chamfered teeth.\"\n\nBuilt in CadXStudio, a browser-based AI text-to-CAD tool. It generates parametric 3D models you can export as STEP, STL or OBJ.\n\nTry it : https://cadxstudio.in\n\n#shorts #CAD #AICAD #TextToCAD #3DPrinting #MechanicalEngineering",
    "content": "Most bad text-to-CAD results are not the AI's fault. They are prompt problems. \"Make a bracket\" gives a model the same freedom it would give a person on their first day: it will guess every dimension, every hole, every wall thickness. A good text-to-CAD prompt works like a short engineering brief.\n\nThis guide gives you a repeatable formula, six before-and-after examples, and the mistakes that waste the most time.\n\n## Why prompts matter more in CAD than in images\n\nAn image can be \"close enough.\" A part cannot. A hole that is 0.5 mm off will not take the bolt. Text-to-CAD tools translate your words into geometry, so every number you leave out is a number the system has to invent. The more of your intent you state, the less you have to fix later.\n\n## The 5-part prompt formula\n\nBuild each prompt from five pieces:\n\n1. **Part and purpose.** What it is and what it does (\"a motor mount for a NEMA 17 stepper\").\n2. **Dimensions.** Overall size and the numbers that matter, with units.\n3. **Features.** Holes, slots, fillets, ribs, bosses, with positions.\n4. **Material and process.** Aluminium sheet, PLA, injection-moulded ABS, and so on. This drives wall thickness and radii.\n5. **Context.** What it mates with, what loads it carries, what must not move.\n\nYou do not need all five every time. For a quick concept, parts 1 and 2 are enough. For something you will send to a printer or a supplier, use all five.\n\n## Weak vs strong prompts\n\n| Weak prompt | Strong prompt |\n| --- | --- |\n| Make a bracket | L-bracket from 3 mm 6061 aluminium sheet. Both legs 40 mm wide, 60 mm long. Two 5.5 mm holes per leg, centred 10 mm from the free edge. Inside bend radius 3 mm. |\n| Design a gear | Spur gear, module 2, 24 teeth, 20° pressure angle, 10 mm face width, 8 mm bore with a 2 mm keyway. |\n| Phone stand | Desk phone stand for phones up to 85 mm wide, 3D printable in PLA without supports, 70° viewing angle, 5 mm wall, cable slot at the rear. |\n| Pipe clamp | Two-piece clamp for 32 mm OD pipe. Two M5 clearance holes, 40 mm apart, 4 mm wall, 12 mm wide band. |\n| Enclosure | Electronics enclosure, 100 × 60 × 30 mm internal, 2.5 mm walls, snap-fit lid, four M3 screw bosses, USB-C cut-out on one short side. |\n| Heat sink | Aluminium heat sink, 50 × 50 mm base 4 mm thick, 12 straight fins 25 mm tall and 1.5 mm thick, evenly spaced. |\n\nNotice what the strong prompts share: units, counts, positions, and one clear manufacturing hint.\n\n## Use Brainstorm before Build\n\nIf you cannot yet write a strong prompt, that is normal. This is why CadXStudio has two modes. In **Brainstorm**, you describe a vague idea and the AI turns it into a structured, detailed spec. You can ask about materials, run calculations, flag design mistakes, and attach reference images. When the spec looks right, switch to **Build** and generate the model.\n\nThink of Brainstorm as the conversation you would have with a senior design engineer before opening CAD, and Build as the modelling step that follows.\n\n## Prompt for how it will be made\n\nTell the system the process, because it changes the geometry. \"3D printed in PETG, no supports, 0.4 mm nozzle\" leads to different choices than \"CNC milled from aluminium\" or \"injection moulded.\" With CadXStudio v2.0 generating DFM-ready models, stating the process and the rules you care about (wall thickness, radii, orientation) helps the result fit how you plan to make it.\n\n## Refine with parameters, not new prompts\n\nAfter Build, resist the urge to re-prompt for every small change. Edit the parameters directly (free in CadXStudio), or use the Manual tool for geometry changes. It is faster, it keeps the rest of the model intact, and it saves AI usage for the steps that need it.\n\n## Five common mistakes\n\n- **No units.** Always write mm, cm, or inches.\n- **Adjectives instead of numbers.** \"Thick\" and \"small\" mean nothing to a CAD kernel.\n- **Stacking ten features in one prompt.** Build the core part first, then add features in steps.\n- **Hiding the constraint.** If it must fit a 25 mm bearing, say so.\n- **Skipping the process.** Printing, milling, and moulding all want different shapes.\n\n## Try it yourself\n\nPick a part you designed last month and rewrite its prompt using the five-part formula. Paste it into [CadXStudio's free plan](https://cadxstudio.in) and compare the result with a one-line prompt. If you can describe it, you can build it.",
