    "id": "35f6eca0-c187-4ce7-9d9a-eef06669ac7f",
    "slug": "from-brainstorm-to-build-designing-a-mechanical-part-end-to-end",
    "title": "From Brainstorm to Build: Designing a Mechanical Part End-to-End",
    "excerpt": "A real walkthrough of designing a mechanical part in CadXStudio — from a rough idea through Brainstorm mode to a finished, exportable Build.",
    "content": "From Brainstorm to Build: Designing a Mechanical Part End-to-End\n\nThe gap between \"I have an idea for a part\" and \"I have a validated, exportable CAD model\" is where most CAD workflows lose the most time. This walkthrough follows that gap end-to-end using CadXStudio's two-stage process — Brainstorm and Build — on a real mechanical part: a desk fan housing.\n\nStarting Point: A Rough Idea\n\nThe starting prompt doesn't need to be precise:\n\n\"I want to design a small desk fan housing that holds a 60mm brushless motor, sits on a weighted base, and can be 3D printed.\"\n\nThis is enough to start, but not enough to finish — and that gap is exactly what Brainstorm mode exists to close.\n\nStage 1: Brainstorm — Turning the Idea Into a Spec\n\nRather than generating geometry immediately from an incomplete description, Brainstorm mode works through the ambiguity first:\n\nClarifying questions: What's the motor's mounting hole pattern? Is the base meant to be removable or integrated? What clearance does the fan blade need from the housing wall?\nMaterial suggestions: For a 3D-printed housing, PETG or ABS get suggested over PLA for a part that will run warm near a motor.\nBasic calculations: Airflow clearance and vent area relative to the fan's diameter.\nDesign mistake flagging: If a wall is specified too thin for the chosen printing process, this stage flags it before any geometry exists — much cheaper to fix here than after printing a failed part.\n\nBy the end of Brainstorm, the vague opening prompt has become a structured, specific spec: exact motor mount pattern, wall thickness suited to the material, vent geometry, and base weight distribution.\n\nStage 2: Build — Generating the Model\n\nWith the spec finalized, Build generates the actual parametric geometry: the housing shell, motor mount bosses, vent pattern, and base interface — as a real editable B-Rep model, not a static render. This is the point where the design becomes something that can be measured, dimensioned, and exported.\n\nStage 3: Refinement\n\nThe first Build output is rarely the final part. Refinement at this stage uses:\n\nParameters — adjusting wall thickness, vent count, or base diameter directly, with the model updating cleanly\nManual tool — for changes that need direct feature-level control, like repositioning a specific mounting boss\n\nThis is deliberately not \"re-prompt and regenerate\" — targeted parametric changes preserve everything about the design that's already correct.\n\nStage 4: Validation and Export\n\nBefore export, the model is checked for basic manufacturability — wall thickness appropriate to the chosen process, interference between the motor and housing, and dimensional accuracy of the mount pattern. Once validated, the model exports as STEP (for further CAD work or manufacturer review) or STL (for slicing and 3D printing).\n\nWhat This Workflow Replaces\n\nIn a traditional CAD workflow, this same part would typically require manually sketching the housing profile, extruding and cutting features one at a time, manually calculating vent area, and often a few physical print-and-measure iterations to catch fit issues. The Brainstorm-to-Build workflow moves several of those iterations earlier — into the spec stage — before any geometry is committed.\n\nFrequently Asked Questions\n\nDo I have to go through Brainstorm mode, or can I jump straight to Build? Brainstorm is optional but strongly reduces rework — it catches ambiguous or conflicting requirements before geometry is generated, rather than after.\n\nCan I attach a reference image during Brainstorm? Yes — image input is supported, letting a reference photo or sketch ground the description alongside the text prompt.\n\nWhat if I need to change the design significantly after Build? Significant changes are usually still faster through targeted parameter or manual edits than a full re-prompt, since re-prompting can alter parts of the design that were already correct.\n\nDoes this workflow work for assemblies, not just single parts? Yes — the same Brainstorm-to-Build flow applies to multi-part assemblies, where individual parts can reference each other's mating geometry within a single workspace.\n\nWhat export formats are available after Build? Standard interoperable formats — STEP, STL, and OBJ — covering CAD interoperability, 3D printing, and general 3D use cases.",
