    "id": "7fdbef90-d074-4ca4-b227-f475d4a6d5dd",
    "slug": "b-rep-vs-mesh-why-it-matters-for-ai-generated-cad",
    "title": "B-Rep vs. Mesh: Why It Matters for AI-Generated CAD",
    "excerpt": "The difference between B-Rep and mesh geometry — and why it determines whether AI-generated 3D models can actually be used for engineering and manufacturing.",
    "content": "B-Rep vs. Mesh: Why It Matters for AI-Generated CAD\n\nNot all AI-generated 3D models are equal, and the difference usually comes down to one technical distinction: is the output a B-Rep (boundary representation) model or a mesh? This single fact determines whether a generated design can be edited, dimensioned, and sent to manufacturing — or whether it's essentially a 3D picture.\n\nWhat Is a Mesh?\n\nA mesh represents a 3D shape as a collection of flat polygons (usually triangles) stitched together to approximate a surface. Meshes are the standard output of most AI 3D-generation tools, game engines, and 3D scanning.\n\nMeshes are good for:\n\nVisualization and rendering\n3D printing (in many cases)\nGames, VFX, and AR/VR\n\nMeshes are poor for:\n\nPrecise dimensioning (a mesh has no concept of \"this hole is 8mm\" — it's just triangles)\nEditing a specific feature without breaking the surrounding surface\nManufacturing processes that require exact tolerances (CNC machining, sheet metal, injection molding)\nWhat Is B-Rep?\n\nB-Rep represents a 3D shape using precise mathematical surfaces, edges, and vertices — the same representation used in every major engineering CAD system (SOLIDWORKS, Fusion 360, Onshape, Siemens NX). A B-Rep model knows that a cylinder is a cylinder with an exact radius, not an approximation built from hundreds of tiny flat faces.\n\nThis means a B-Rep model can be:\n\nDimensioned exactly (a hole is 8.000mm, not \"approximately 8mm made of triangles\")\nEdited parametrically — change one dimension and the model updates cleanly\nExported to STEP, the standard interoperability format for real manufacturing and other CAD tools\nWhy This Matters for AI CAD Specifically\n\nBecause AI CAD generation is new, it's tempting to assume all \"AI-generated 3D models\" are the same category of output. They aren't. A tool that generates a mesh from a text prompt is doing something closer to AI image generation extended into 3D — impressive to look at, but not usable for engineering without a full manual rebuild.\n\nA tool that generates true B-Rep geometry, like CadXStudio, is producing something a mechanical engineer can actually use: a model with real feature history, real editable dimensions, and a real STEP export that a manufacturer or downstream CAD tool can open and trust.\n\nHow to Tell Which One You're Looking At\n\nA few practical tests:\n\nTry to change a dimension. If you can update a hole diameter or wall thickness and the model updates cleanly, it's B-Rep. If you have to manually push and pull triangle vertices, it's mesh.\nCheck the export options. STEP export is a strong signal of B-Rep. STL-only export is common for mesh-based tools (though STL can also be exported from a B-Rep model for printing).\nLook for a feature tree or history. Parametric feature history (extrude, fillet, pattern, as a sequence of edits) only exists in B-Rep systems.\nFrequently Asked Questions\n\nIs STL a B-Rep format or a mesh format? STL is a mesh format — it describes a shape as a set of triangles, with no dimensional or feature information. It's commonly used for 3D printing but isn't suitable for editing or manufacturing tolerances that require true geometric precision.\n\nCan a B-Rep model be converted to a mesh? Yes — this is a one-way conversion. A B-Rep model can be exported as an STL mesh for 3D printing or visualization. A mesh cannot be reliably converted back into a fully editable B-Rep model without significant manual rework.\n\nWhy do some AI CAD tools only produce mesh output? Generating accurate B-Rep geometry from a text prompt is a harder technical problem than generating a mesh, since it requires the system to reason about exact dimensions, feature relationships, and manufacturability — not just an approximate shape.\n\nDoes B-Rep output mean a design is automatically manufacturable? No. B-Rep means the geometry is precise and editable — it still needs to be validated for wall thickness, tolerances, and manufacturing method (3D printing, machining, sheet metal) before it's truly ready for production.\n\nWhich CAD systems use B-Rep? Essentially all professional parametric CAD systems -SOLIDWORKS, Fusion 360, Onshape, Siemens NX, CATIA — use B-Rep as their core geometry representation, which is why B-Rep output from an AI CAD tool is interoperable with the rest of the engineering CAD ecosystem.",
