    "excerpt": "Most \"AI CAD\" tools generate meshes, not manufacturable geometry. This is a technical breakdown of why that distinction matters  B-rep vs. mesh, feature trees vs. static shapes, and what it actually takes for AI-generated parts to survive contact with a machine shop.",
    "content": "# What Is AI CAD? A Technical Guide to Parametric Design Intelligence in 2026\n\nThe term \"AI CAD\" is used loosely enough now that it's lost most of its meaning. To engineers evaluating these tools for real work, the label tells you almost nothing about whether the output can survive contact with a machine shop. This is a technical breakdown of what actually separates AI-generated geometry that's manufacturable from geometry that only looks manufacturable.\n\n## The Core Distinction: Boundary Representation vs. Mesh\n\nEvery 3D model produced by an \"AI CAD\" tool falls into one of two fundamentally different data structures.\n\n**Mesh geometry** (the output of most generative AI 3D tools, including diffusion-based systems trained on visual data) represents a shape as a collection of discrete triangular or polygonal faces. A mesh can be watertight and visually convincing, but it has no concept of a true surface equation, no feature history, and no design intent. A cylindrical hole in a mesh isn't a cylinder  it's an approximation built from dozens of flat triangles. There's no way to query \"what is the diameter of this hole\" as a parametric value, because the hole isn't a hole; it's a set of coordinates that happen to look like one.\n\n**Boundary representation (B-rep)** — the data structure used by every mainstream CAD kernel (Parasolid, ACIS, Open CASCADE) represents a solid as an exact topological structure of faces, edges, and vertices, where each face is defined by an underlying analytic or NURBS surface equation. A cylindrical hole in a B-rep model is an actual cylinder: a face with a defined radius, axis, and extent, editable and dimensionable as a first-class parameter.\n\nThis distinction is not cosmetic. It determines whether a model can be:\n- Dimensioned and toleranced (GD&T requires exact analytic surfaces, not triangle approximations)\n- Edited by changing a single parameter without destroying the rest of the model\n- Exported to STEP (AP203/AP214/AP242) in a form a CAM system or supplier can trust\n- Checked for manufacturability draft angles, wall thickness, undercuts against exact geometry rather than a faceted approximation\n\nAn AI tool that generates meshes and calls itself \"CAD\" is solving a visualization problem, not an engineering problem. STL is a mesh format and is fine for 3D printing preview, but it was never designed to carry design intent, and reverse-engineering a parametric model from a mesh (a process called \"B-rep conversion\" or \"surface fitting\") is lossy, unreliable, and not something any team should build a production workflow around.\n\n## How AI CAD Systems Actually Generate Geometry\n\nThere are two structurally different approaches being marketed under the same \"AI CAD\" umbrella, and they produce different classes of output:\n\n**1. Diffusion/generative mesh models** — trained on large datasets of 3D visual assets, these systems generate shape the way image diffusion models generate pixels: statistically, from noise, optimizing for visual plausibility. The output is a mesh. It can look impressive and even proportionally correct, but it carries no feature tree, no constraint history, and no analytic surface definitions. It is, structurally, a piece of digital sculpture.\n\n**2. LLM-driven parametric construction** — these systems use a language model to reason about design intent and translate a prompt into a sequence of native CAD kernel operations: sketch a profile, apply a constraint, extrude, fillet an edge, boolean-union with another body, pattern an array of holes. The output is the same class of object a human engineer would produce clicking through SolidWorks or Fusion a real feature tree, built on an actual B-rep kernel, that happens to have been authored by a model instead of a hand.\n\nThe second approach is fundamentally harder to build well, because it requires the model to reason correctly about geometric constraints, coordinate frames, boolean operation order, and manufacturing logic not just \"what should this look like\" but \"what sequence of exact operations produces a valid, non-self-intersecting, manifold solid that matches this intent.\" It's also the only approach that produces something an engineer can actually open, edit, and hand off downstream.\n\n## Why This Matters for Tolerancing and Assemblies\n\nReal parts don't exist in isolation — they mate with other parts, and those relationships have to survive dimensional variation. This is where the mesh/B-rep distinction compounds:\n\n- **GD&T (geometric dimensioning and tolerancing)** requires true datums and exact analytic surfaces. You cannot apply a positional tolerance to a triangle-approximated hole with any rigor.\n- **Multi-body assemblies** require consistent coordinate origins and defined mating relationships between parts. A model that \"reasons about design intent\" needs to track where Part B's mounting face sits relative to Part A's datum not just generate two shapes that look adjacent in a render.\n- **Boolean operations** (union, subtract, intersect) between bodies are only numerically stable on B-rep geometry with well-defined topology. Attempting boolean operations on two independently-generated meshes frequently produces non-manifold garbage gaps, overlapping faces, inverted normals that has to be manually repaired before the part is usable.\n\nAn AI CAD system intended for real engineering work has to get all three of these right simultaneously, across every generated feature, not just produce a single isolated shape that looks correct from one angle.\n\n## A Technical Checklist for Evaluating AI CAD Tools\n\nThree tests that separate genuine parametric CAD generation from mesh generation with a CAD label:\n\n1. **Export a STEP file and reopen it in a traditional CAD system.** If the faces are faceted rather than analytic surfaces, or if the feature tree is missing entirely, the tool produced a mesh, not a model.\n2. **Change one dimension after generation.** In true parametric CAD, editing a hole diameter or extrusion depth should update only the dependent geometry, not require regenerating the entire part from scratch.\n3. **Generate a two-part assembly with a mating constraint.** Check whether the coordinate origins are consistent and the parts actually align this reveals whether the system reasons about assembly-level design intent or just generates isolated shapes independently.\n\n## Where This Is Heading\n\nThe next phase of this category isn't about making generation faster most systems can already produce a shape in seconds. The harder, more consequential problem is closing the loop between generation and validation: AI systems that reason",
